## Part 2: Advanced WAF and Firewall Bypasses
Most mature corporate targets deploy a Web Application Firewall (WAF) or simple string-matching filters that inspect incoming requests. If they see standard indicators like 169.254.169.254 or localhost, they will block the connection instantly.
Here are the advanced methods used to bypass these input restriction filters:
## 1. Hexadecimal, Octal, and Decimal Conversion
Computers understand IP addresses as numbers. Many internal network libraries will automatically translate alternative mathematical formats back into standard IPs, bypassing naive filters that look exclusively for text containing periods.

* Decimal Representation: Convert the whole IP address into a single continuous sequence of integers.

http://169.254.169  (Equivalent to 169.254.169.254)
http://127.0.0                  (Equivalent to 127.0.0.1 / localhost)

* Hexadecimal Notation: Translate the numbers into base-16 structures.

http://169.254.169  (Equivalent to 169.254.169.254)
http://127.0.0                  (Equivalent to 127.0.0.1)

* Octal Notation: Pad the byte chunks with leading zeroes to interpret them as base-8 numbers.

http://0251.0376.0251.0376/latest/meta-data/ (Equivalent to 169.254.169.254)


## 2. Exploiting DNS Rebinding (The Timed Swap)
If the application validates your input by running a DNS resolution lookup on the backend before fetching the file, it will see a safe, public IP address and allow the request to pass. However, if you manipulate the DNS server to change its answer on the next lookup, the server will fetch the internal cloud metadata instead.
You can use a free, public automation sandbox tool like rbndr.us to generate a dynamic DNS name that alternates between a public safe IP and a private blocked IP:

* Go to the rebinding utility profile and bind two IPs together (e.g., Google's public IP 8.8.8.8 and the AWS metadata IP 169.254.169.254).
* It will give you a custom domain name link like 7f000001.08080808.rbndr.us.
* Inject this domain name directly into your target parameter:

https://target.com

* The Result: The target server checks the domain, sees 8.8.8.8, and thinks the request is safe. When it goes to download the webpage a fraction of a second later, the DNS cache expires, the domain resolves to 169.254.169.254, and the server dumps its internal cloud security keys directly into your lap.

